issue(id="0008" status="open")
  title Add validation boundaries and useful formatter error reporting
  user-story
    as-a user of puggers
    i-want useful errors when syntax is malformed or unsupported
    so-that I can trust the tool and quickly understand when it is preserving versus rejecting input
  premise.
    The current formatter leans toward preservation and idempotence, which is
    appropriate for bootstrapping, but it also means malformed or unsupported
    syntax can silently pass through without clear boundaries. As the syntax
    model grows, the tool should become more intentional about what it accepts,
    what it preserves opaquely, and what it reports as an error. This will also
    matter for a future CLI or npm-facing developer experience.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Define the categories of syntax handling: fully supported, preserved but not normalized, and unsupported or invalid.
    criterion Add useful error messages for malformed indentation and clearly broken syntax once the relevant constructs are structured.
    criterion Preserve lossless fallback behavior where practical, but make that behavior explicit rather than accidental.
    criterion Add tests that cover both successful preservation and intentional failures.
  review-items
    item(status="open") Decide whether validation should be strict by default or whether permissive preservation remains the default mode.
    item(status="open") Confirm how error messages should surface source location information for both the dprint plugin and the standalone tooling.
    item(status="open") Avoid making unsupported syntax look successfully normalized when it was actually passed through untouched.

